First convert each hourly wage to U.S. dollars.

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Navin:}&\; \frac{160\ \text{MUR}}{32.35\ \text{MUR/USD}} = 4.944\ \text{USD/hr}\\[4pt]
\text{Luka:}&\; \frac{25\ \text{HRK}}{5.18\ \text{HRK/USD}} = 4.826\ \text{USD/hr}\\[4pt]
\text{Ian:}&\; \frac{34\ \text{BWP}}{6.95\ \text{BWP/USD}} = 4.892\ \text{USD/hr}
\end{aligned}
\]

All three work the same 8‑hour day, so the total earnings are proportional to the hourly rates. The highest hourly rate is Navin’s \(4.944\) USD/hr.

\[
\boxed{\text{Navin}}
\]

Using the hourly rates already converted to U.S. dollars:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Navin:}&\;4.944\ \text{USD/hr}\times 8\ \text{hr}=39.552\ \text{USD}\\
\text{Luka:}&\;4.826\ \text{USD/hr}\times 8\ \text{hr}=38.608\ \text{USD}\\
\text{Ian:}&\;4.892\ \text{USD/hr}\times 8\ \text{hr}=39.136\ \text{USD}
\end{aligned}
\]

Navin’s total ($39.552) is the greatest.

\[
\boxed{\text{Navin}}
\]

